Palm's Messaging (com.palm.app.messaging), as webOS Community Edition ships it, copied from
/usr/palm/applications on the reference TouchPad (webOS CE 3.1.0), where it is 3.0.6607. Its
version is 3.2.0 here, the number webOS CE gives it for release (codepoet, 2026-10-10). It is HP webOS
3.0.5's Messaging 3.0.6606 with the webOS CE project's changes (by Herrie and the webOS CE
community): servers and channels, reactions, edits and deletions, receipts, failed-message
dashboards, and Unicode emoji drawn from the bundled images.

Copyright Palm, Inc. / Hewlett-Packard. Never released under an open licence; distributed by
Lunacy as abandonware, like Mojo, Email and the Prelude fonts. The webOS CE changes are the webOS
CE project's.

images/emoji: the EmojiOne set, as webOS CE bundled it (utilities/utils.js names it). EmojiOne's
free releases were licensed under Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 (CC BY 4.0); the version
isn't recorded in the app. Emoji artwork by EmojiOne (Ranks.com, now JoyPixels).

The app is here as it was installed, with that version number and less one file: debug.log, a Windows Chrome crash log left in
the CE package, and for one change codepoet approved (rule 5 in CLAUDE.md, 2026-11-04): on a
phone's card, where enyo.SlidingPane shows one panel at a time, a picked conversation or New
Conversation slides the chat panel over the list, and back slides it away. The hooks are marked
[Lunacy] in app/MessagingApp.js and app/threads/ThreadList.js; they change nothing on a
TouchPad or a tablet, and have to be carried over when CE's Messaging is taken again.

A second change, codepoet's, 2026-11-04, for CE to take too (docs/messaging.md, "For webOS CE
to take"): app/buddies/BuddyService.js, marked [Lunacy], finds the conversation of a buddy
that has no contacts person by its address, rather than matching it to every conversation
that has no person and showing one of their unread counts.
